class Solution {
  public int[] roundDeskProofStrip(int[] readings, int k) {
    int[] a=readings; List<Integer> out=new ArrayList<>();
    {int[]remaining=a.clone(),done=new int[a.length];Deque<Integer>queue=new ArrayDeque<>();for(int i=0;i<a.length;i++)if(a[i]>0)queue.add(i);int time=0;while(!queue.isEmpty()){int i=queue.remove(),served=Math.min(k,remaining[i]);remaining[i]-=served;time+=served;if(remaining[i]>0)queue.add(i);else done[i]=time;}for(int x:done)out.add(x);}
    return out.stream().mapToInt(Integer::intValue).toArray();
  }
}

privateData.editorialMd:
Build the result list

Each number in readings is the work time needed by a job. All jobs are ready at time 0, in input order. The worker gives the first job up to k time units. If it still needs work, put it at the back of the line. Then serve the next job. Record when each job finishes, measured from time 0. Return these finish times in original job order. A job needing 0 time finishes at time 0 and uses no worker time.

Return the answer

Return the whole result list in the order you built it. If it is empty, return [].

Why this works

Each value in the result list follows Step 1. Step 2 uses only those values. Keeping these steps separate helps avoid returning the list when the question asks for one number. Check empty input, one item, repeated values, and the largest allowed input.

Efficiency goal: time O(n + total/k); extra memory, including the result list, O(n). n is the input length; total is all job work time added together. These labels describe how work and memory grow with the input.

